The dados.gov team participates in regular events, such as conferences, with various audiences (public-sector, academia and public in general). An important annual event about data is organized by the Data & Interoperability team from the agency AMA (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cB3uyZ_Nfn0&ab_channel=AMA-Ag%C3%AAnciaparaaModerniza%C3%A7%C3%A3oAdministrativa).
Some examples of the more important events are:
10º Forum de Gestão de Dados de Investigação conference , https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dBJYSNdd01k;
JIIDE2023 conference, https://www.dgterritorio.gov.pt/jiide2023/ - https://www.youtube.com/@JIIDE2023;
ENIIG conference, https://eniig.dgterritorio.gov.pt/2023/inicio.

For the three types of datasets registered in dados.gov - manually, via API, and Harvesting - the following applies:
For manual registration in dados.gov, the uData framework defines the minimum metadata that must be filled in;
Via API, the payload of the request is defined (fully documented in the reference provided in dados.gov), for example for a POST the minimum metadata is, created_at, description, frequency, last_modified, last_update, url, slug, title and uri;
For the harvesting process, which we cannot control, the framework controls a mapping to the provider’s metadata standard.

In addition to the guidelines and regular meetings with providers to assist publishers in supplying high-quality datasets, the dados.gov team participates in regular events, such as conferences, with various audiences. An important annual event about data is organized by the Data & Interoperability team from the agency AMA (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cB3uyZ_Nfn0&ab_channel=AMA-Ag%C3%AAnciaparaaModerniza%C3%A7%C3%A3oAdministrativa). Some examples of these conferences are:
10º Forum de Gestão de Dados de Investigação conference , https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dBJYSNdd01k;
JIIDE2023 conference, https://www.dgterritorio.gov.pt/jiide2023/ - https://www.youtube.com/@JIIDE2023;
ENIIG conference, https://eniig.dgterritorio.gov.pt/2023/inicio.

Explanation from 2023:
At AMA there is an ongoing project entitled"Dados Abertos - Definição de modelo de avaliação de impacto" (Open Data- impact assessment model definition; URL https://www.base.gov.pt/Base4/pt/detalhe/?type=contratos&id=9601046) funded under the Recovery and Resilience Plan (Digital Transition). The aim of such project is to:
1) Compile an international benchmark of methodologies, use cases and best practices to assess the impact (economic, social, etc.) of using open data;
2) Define metrics for impact assessment and monitoring;
3) Stimulate cooperation among public administration (PA) entities (central, local and regional) in joint initiatives to make open data available and reused;
4) Develop and communicate metrics and indicators of success for data publication by PA entities
5) Demonstrate the value of open data, with simple reuse cases (e.g. visualizations) of datasets published by entities registered in data.gov, chosen by the potential to generate benefits and innovation, and relevance of this information;
6) Identify criteria and model for evaluating the quality of datasets highlighting good practices in the publication of datasets
At the present moment, this project has already done itens 1 and 2 and is working on case studies

Provide updated explanation (if applicab:
At AMA there is an ongoing project entitled"Dados Abertos - Definição de modelo de avaliação de impacto" (Open Data- impact assessment model definition; URL https://www.base.gov.pt/Base4/pt/detalhe/?type=contratos&id=9601046) funded under the Recovery and Resilience Plan (Digital Transition). The aim of the project is to:
1) Compile an international benchmark of methodologies, use cases and best practices to assess the impact (economic, social, etc.) of using open data;
2) Define metrics for impact assessment and monitoring;
3) Stimulate cooperation among public administration (PA) entities (central, local and regional) in joint initiatives to make open data available and reused;
4) Develop and communicate metrics and indicators of success for data publication by PA entities
5) Demonstrate the value of open data, with simple reuse cases (e.g. visualisations) of datasets published by entities registered in data.gov, chosen by the potential to generate benefits and innovation, and relevance of this information;
6) Identify criteria and model for evaluating the quality of datasets, highlighting good practices in the publication of datasets
This project is ongoing, and the impact measurement methodology has already been applied to different case studies in the country. The methodology is based on surveys to data providers and reusers. It assesses the acknowledgement, knowledge and evidence of the impact of open data in four different areas: governmental, economic, social, and environmental. Future steps include increasing the number of case studies and entities participating in the project. More details on this project can be on the file attached to this survey_Relatório Casos de Estudo.pdf

1- The use of municipal open data repositories of liquid and atmospheric pollutents (URL: https://urbandatalab.pt/index.php/component/sppagebuilder/?view=page&id=119) enables the elaboration of public-available predictive models of the propagation of pollutants that impact air and water quality. In other words, the "objective of these predictive tools is to simulate different pollutant emission scenarios allowing the creation of emergency measures to minimize the impact of an eventual hazard on public health and other citizen's life aspects."; 
2- The Waste water quality open data and re-use (URL: https://www.ersar.pt/pt/consumidor/qualidade-da-agua) creates awareness about the waste water and can be used to infer the health of the population; 
3- The Portuguese Environment Agency (APA) creates the Info Praia app for the rapid availability of up-to-date information on beaches and the quality of coastal, transitional and inland bathing waters. - https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=pt.apambiente.info_praia&hl=pt_PT&gl=US; Related Open Datasets URLs: https://dados.gov.pt/pt/datasets/rh4-zonas-de-risco-de-erosao/; https://dados.gov.pt/pt/datasets/praias-com-bandeira-azul-n-o/; https://dados.gov.pt/pt/datasets/instrucoes-para-colheita-de-amostras-de-aguas-balneares/

There are 4 areas of open data that are available and that may be influencing the market and be interesting for those who follow the renewables sector, namely:
- E-Redes: provides a website with it's data. One of the important examples for the renewables implementation is the availability of installed power at transformation stations and its use, which helps in the planning the of network and connections. For example, for industrial, tt is possible to know whether or not power is available in the grids at a certain area. The same applies to residential areas (https://e-redes.opendatasoft.com/explore/dataset/postos-transformacao-distribuicao/mapa/). It is available in English;
- OMIE: Energy values
